{"chunk_id": "63f4a895f4c4", "text": "This section follows the controversial killing of the teacher Drona by Dhrishtadyumna. Bhima and Dhrishtadyumna defend the act, arguing that Drona had abandoned the duties of a Brahmana and fought as a destructive warrior, making his death justified in war. Arjuna, however, is pained by the killing of his former preceptor and reproaches Dhrishtadyumna. Dhritarashtra laments the humiliation and death of the great teacher and asks how the warriors reacted. Tensions erupt in the Pandava camp: Satyaki furiously condemns Dhrishtadyumna for murdering his own teacher, accusing him of a terrible sin. Dhrishtadyumna counters by accusing Satyaki of hypocrisy and listing many morally ambiguous acts committed by both sides during the war. The argument escalates until Satyaki rushes to attack him, only to be physically restrained by Bhima and then calmed by Sahadeva, highlighting the moral chaos and internal fractures caused by the war.\nAfter Drona\u2019s death, Dhrishtadyumna defends killing his teacher while Satyaki condemns it, sparking a furious quarrel nearly turning allies against each other.\nThemes: moral ambiguity of war, revenge and justice, guru-slaying controversy, allied conflict, kshatriya duty", "vector": [-0.0018101006280630827, -0.00798197090625763, -0.01946953684091568, -0.011642620898783207, -0.012950478121638298, 0.023460522294044495, -0.03133462741971016, 0.005757266189903021, -0.01507405936717987, -0.027613304555416107, 0.007031415123492479, 0.014939229004085064, 0.014103279449045658, 0.0014342603972181678, 0.006512317340821028, 0.0055887275375425816, 0.048646874725818634, 0.015303271822631359, 0.02352793700993061, -0.031064966693520546, -0.01199992187321186, -0.011231387965381145, -0.0037786271423101425, 0.02847621962428093, -0.008635899052023888, 0.028718914836645126, 0.005039293318986893, -0.03179305046796799, 0.013887550681829453, -0.014049346558749676, -0.009262860752642155, -0.016530228778719902, -0.0037718855310